  PASS  1  one request: first token and finish times match the closed form
  PASS  2  a one-token output finishes with its prefill iteration (idle clock jumps to the arrival)
  PASS  3  continuous with one slot reproduces static batch-size-1 exactly (60 requests)
  PASS  4  24 runs: every admitted request emits exactly `out` tokens; block accounting invariant holds each iteration (832 preemptions exercised)
  PASS  5  heavy memory pressure: terminates, all blocks returned (90 preemptions)
  PASS  6  a request larger than the whole pool is rejected up front; others proceed
  PASS  7  without preemption, total prefill work equals the sum of prompt lengths
  PASS  8  with preemption, prefill work exceeds the prompt total (recomputation is real work)
  PASS  9  chunked prefill: per-iteration work stays inside max_batched_tokens (bounded step time)
  PASS 10  strict FCFS: first-token times are non-decreasing in arrival order (no preemption)
  PASS 11  same seed, same result
  PASS 12  static batch: the short request is done early, but the finished slot still costs compute
  PASS 13  continuous batching throughput >= static (N=32) on 5 of 5 seeds
  PASS 14  block count after L tokens equals ceil(L / B), as in the Chapter 43 cache
ALL PASS   (1 s)  [14 tests]
assumed cost model: iteration = 15 ms + 50.00 ms per 1000 prefill tokens + 0.20 ms per decoding sequence
SLO: TTFT <= 2 s and mean time per output token <= 80 ms
workload: prompt median 256 (mean 351), output median 128 (mean 199)
decode-only upper bound with 128 sequences (ignores prefill and ramp): 3153 tokens/s = 15.8 requests/s

